What's all this about Gordon, Petty?\" the sergeant growled. \"So familiar.\" [982] \"All right, all right, sergeant,\" J. R. Rine interceded. \"Don't let your personal feelings get in the way of the discussion. You know you're an old fossil. Young people aren't like they used to be.\" [983] \"Dad,\" Peasmarsh blushed. [984] \"Not like your time,\" Roarke added. [985] \"Introductions, flirting, kissing in dark corners,\" Rine continued with a smile. \"Well, sergeant, you have to accept these things. I said Clurman was a slippery character, trying to cover his tracks, but there are some strange things going on that need looking into.\" [986] \"Well, anyway,\" the sergeant grumbled, \"I still don't like... huh? I didn't think of that. If so, I think we should ask Klabber a few questions.\" [987] Pencils examined the cigarette butt, his voice low: \"Dad, you know, this makes me think. Why don't we investigate Dr. Sedlar first instead of bothering Mr. Klabber?\" [988] \"Patti, you mean Britain?\" [989] \"Let's not be greedy. How about the shipping company?\" [990] \"Shipping company? What's that supposed to mean?\" [991] \"Just to see if there's any drama,\" Peachins murmured. [992] Forty-five minutes later, the sergeant put down the phone, wiping his brow with a trembling handkerchief. He finally sighed: \"Well, the show's about to start. It's too absurd... You know what the shipping company's manager just told me?\" [993] \"Oh, Dad,\" Peachins said, \"you're teasing us. What did he say?\" [994] \"There's no Hamnet Sedra on the passenger list!\" [995] They all looked at each other in surprise. Then Gordon Rowe whistled, putting out his cigarette in the sergeant's ashtray. \"So that's it,\" he murmured. \"The famous Dr. Sedra...\"\n[996] \"Good, I like it,\" Percy murmured. \"I like it a lot.\" [997] \"Good heavens, he's a fake!\" Sam exclaimed, \"Listen, kid, you can't tell a soul. Not a word, or else I'll\u2014\"\n[998] \"Alright, alright, sergeant,\" Reine interrupted him gently, leaning back on the leather sofa with many wrinkles on his forehead, \"Don't be in such a hurry. A good set-up doesn't necessarily make a good play, and a suspicious situation can't convict someone. Why did you describe Sedra to the secretary?\" [999] \"Well,\" Sam snorted, \"he checked the passenger list and couldn't find the guy's name. I described Sedra to the secretary and asked him to check with the ship's staff. The ship docked this morning, and they're all still on duty, so he did it right away. Well, Sedra not only isn't on the passenger list, but no one remembers anyone on board who looks like Sedra!\" [1000] \"Interesting,\" Rowe pondered. [1001] \"I admit the strange smell has grown stronger,\" muttered the old gentleman, \"strange, very strange\u2026\"\n[1002] \"Can't you see what this means?\" Percy exclaimed, \"It means that Sedra has been in the country at least four days!\" [1003] \"How did you figure that out, Percy?\" his father asked. [1004] \"He didn't fly across the Atlantic, did he? You remember I called the shipping company last Thursday to ask when the next ship from Britain was due in port - Sally Bostwick wrote to me saying she was coming, but she didn't say when. They said there was a ship on Saturday, the next one was today, and there were no other ships due in port. Since today is Tuesday, I figured this British fellow must have been in New York at least four days - at least since last Saturday.\" [1005] \"Even longer,\" Rowe frowned, \"Sedra! Unbelievable!\" [1006] \"You might check the Saturday ship,\" Raine said casually. [1007] The sergeant reached for the phone, then sat back down. \"I have a better idea, kill two birds with one stone,\" he pressed a button, and Miss Brody appeared in the office as if by magic, \"Did you bring your notebook? Good, send a telegram to Scotland Yard!\" [1008] \"To \u2013 to whom, Sergeant?\" Miss Brody stammered, surprised to see the tall, strong young man at the door. [1009] \"Scotland Yard. I want this slippery fellow to see how we do things here!\" The sergeant's face turned red, \"Don't you know where Scotland Yard is? London, England!\" [1010] \"Yes \u2013 yes, sir,\" Miss Brody hastily replied. [1011] \"Mark it for Inspector Trench, T-r-e-n-c-h. 'Request all information on the former director of the London Golden Museum, Hamnet Sedlar, now in New York. Include departure date from Britain, physical description, social connections, reputation, and any other available information. Keep confidential. Best regards. Send immediately.'\" [1012] Miss Brawdy stumbled towards Rowley. [1013] \"Wait, how did you spell Sedlar's name?\" [1014] \"S-e-d-d-l-e-r,\" Miss Brawdy stammered, her face pale with excitement. [1015] The inspector took a deep breath and smiled. \"Good, good, Miss Brawdy,\" he said calmly, \"Don't faint. It's all right. Good heavens, can't you even spell? It's S-e-d-l-a-r!\" [1016] \"Oh, yes, sir.\" Miss Broude quickly ran away. [1017] \"Poor Miss Broude,\" Pecchini chuckled. \"Dad, you always scare her like that. Maybe it's because of the strange young man around... Oh, Mr. Lane, what's wrong?\" She spoke with caution. [1018] Lane looked surprised and stared at Sam as if he had never seen him before, or as if he wasn't looking at him, and then jumped up. [1019] \"Good heavens!\" he exclaimed. \"So that's it!\" He paced around the room, muttering to himself. [1020] \"What's going on?\" the inspector asked, surprised. [1021] \"This name! This name! Hamnet Sedley... Good heavens, it's... it's... unbelievable! If it's a coincidence, then there's no justice in the world.\" [1022] \"Name?\" Percy frowned. \"What's so strange about a name, Mr. Lane? It sounds like proper English to me.\" [1023] Gordon Rowe's mouth hung open, like a digger, and the mischievous glint in his brown eyes was replaced by a look of astonishment and comprehension. [1024] Mr. Lane stopped pacing, rubbing his chin, and burst out in a series of low chuckles. \"Of course, it's proper English, Percy. You have a knack for getting to the heart of the matter. You're absolutely right. Not only English, but historical English. Ha! Gordon, I see your eyes shining. He stopped laughing, suddenly sitting down, and spoke in a serious tone, \"I knew I had heard that name before.\" [1025] The inspector looked blank. \"I haven't heard of any trouble.\" [1026] \"Well, Percy, you're more educated than your father. You've studied British literature, haven't you?\" [1027] \"Of course.\" [1028] \"Have you studied Elizabethan literature?\" [1029] Percy's cheeks flushed. \"That\u2014that was a long time ago.\" [1030] The old gentleman shook his head dejectedly. \"Typical modern education. So you've never heard of Hamnet Sedley. Gordon, tell them who Hamnet Sedley is!\" [1031] \"Hamnet Sedley\u2014\" Mr. Rowe's voice was a bit stunned, \"one of William Shakespeare's closest friends.\" [1032] \"Shakespeare!\" Sam exclaimed, \"Rayne, are you mistaken? Are you all crazy? What does old Shakespeare have to do with this?\" [1033] \"It has a lot to do with it. I began to think\u2026\" Rayne whispered, \"Right, Gordon, yes. It's just\u2026\" He pondered, shaking his head, \"You must know. Sedley\u2026 Oh my!\" [1034] \"I'm still not quite getting it,\" Percy complained. \"In that regard, I agree with my father. Of course\u2026\"\n[1035] \"This Sedley guy must be a wandering Jew, right?\" the sergeant joked. \"He can't be over three hundred years old, can he?\" He laughed merrily. [1036] \"Ha, ha!\" Mr. Rawlins sighed deeply. [1037] \"I don't mean our friend is Ahasuerus (2),\" Mr. Raine smiled, \"So far, nothing has been so exaggerated. But what I mean is, Dr. Hamnet Sadler - former director of the Kingston Museum in London, future director of the British Museum in New York, English, cultured, a book collector... Oh, Dr. Sadler is a direct descendant of Shakespeare's friend, it's not completely impossible.\" [1038] \"The family from Stratford?\" Percyns pondered. [1039] The old man shrugged, \"We know nothing about them.\" [1040] \"I think -\" Rawlins said slowly, \"The Sadler family comes from Gloucestershire.\" [1041] \"What difference does that make?\" Pecham protested. \"If Dr. Sedgwick is the descendant of Shakespeare's close friend, does the Sedgwick family have anything to do with this copy of the Jagger edition of 'The Passionate Pilgrim' from 1599, which caused all this commotion?\" [1042] Mr. Reen said quietly: \"Dear, the problem is here. Inspector, you sent a telegram to your British friends at Scotland Yard, and it gave us such inspiration. Maybe we can find out... who knows? 'The Passionate Pilgrim' itself is impossible... but that... \" He fell silent again. [1043] The inspector sat helplessly, looking at his friends and then at his daughter. Young Gordon Rowe stared at Reen, and Pecham stared at Rowe. [1044] Reen suddenly stood up and reached for his cane. They watched him silently. [1045] \"Strange,\" he said. \"Very strange.\" He nodded, lost in thought, and left the inspector's office. [1046] --- --- --- --- ---\n[1047] (1) Fran\u00e7ois Villon (1431-1463), a French poet known for his lyrical works, was often involved in drunken brawls, fights, and theft, committing numerous crimes. [1048] (2) Ahasuerus, a Persian king mentioned in the Bible. [1049] Chapter 11\n[1050] 3HS wM\n[1051]Dromio cheerfully muttered curses at the traffic policeman as he drove the black Lincoln away from Fifth Avenue. He skillfully maneuvered the car through the maze of vehicles, pulling up to a red light on Sixth Avenue. [1052]Mr. Jury sat silently in the back seat, tapping the sharp edge of a yellow sheet of paper against his lips. He had read the words written on the paper over a dozen times, his brow furrowed. It was a telegram, dated \"June 21 - 12:06 AM.\" The telegram had been delivered to Hamlet House in Westchester at dawn. [1053]\"It's strange for Sam to wire me at this hour,\" the old man thought to himself. \"In the middle of the night! It couldn't be... an emergency? Not likely...\"\n[1054]Dromio leaned over to honk the horn. A car and another were locked in a tangle at the corner, refusing to yield, with a line of cars behind them, the situation truly dismal. Jury looked ahead and saw the chaos spread to Fifth Avenue, so he leaned forward and tapped Dromio's ear. [1055]\"I think I'll walk the rest of the way,\" he said. \"Just one block. Meet me at Sam's office.\" [1056] He got out of the car, still holding the telegram, and carefully placed it in his coat pocket before walking towards Broadway. [1057] He found Sam Detective Agency in disarray. Miss Broadly sat in the reception area, her tense expression mirroring the chaos around her, her eyes sorrowfully fixed on Percy. Percy paced behind the railing, looking like an enraged sergeant, biting his lip and urgently glancing at the clock on the wall. [1058] At the sound of the door opening, she jumped up, Miss Broadly letting out a soft shriek. [1059] \"You've finally arrived!\" Percy exclaimed, gripping the old gentleman's arm tightly. \"I thought you'd never come. You're a lifesaver!\" [1060] Lean was startled as Percy embraced him, his arms wrapping around his neck, and passionately kissed his cheek. \"Dear child,\" Lean protested, \"you're trembling! What's happened? The telegram from the commissioner was so cryptic, it didn't tell me anything. Is he alright?\" [1061] \"He's doing wonderfully,\" Percy sneered, her eyes brightening as she touched the earrings on her ear, \"Now, let's get to the body!\" She pushed open the door to the chief's office, revealing an elderly, haggard gentleman with red-rimmed eyes and a pale complexion, stiffly sitting in a swivel chair, staring determinedly at the items on the desk. [1062] \"Hooray!\" he exclaimed, staggering to his feet, \"Good Lord! Pet, I told you you could count on the old coot! Sit down, Mr. Reen, sit down. It's so good to see you.\" [1063] Mr. Reen sank into the leather sofa. \"Goodness, what a welcome! I feel like a prodigal son. All right, tell me what's going on. I'm about to burst with curiosity.\" [1064] Sam picked up his meticulously studied item. \"See this?\" [1065] \"Well, my eyesight is perfectly fine, so I saw it.\" [1066] The inspector grinned. \"Alright, we're going to open it.\" [1067] Rayne looked from Sam to Peachins. \"But \u2014 alright, do as you please. Inspector, is that why you called me here?\" [1068] \"We sent you a telegram,\" Peachins blurted out.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nA few years ago, William Siddra was actively involved in book collecting in Britain and had a close friendship with the famous British book collector, Sir Humphrey. When Samuel Saxon bought a 1599 Jaggard edition of \"The Passionate Pilgrim\" from Sir Humphrey, William played a significant role. William always had easy access to Sir Humphrey's remarkable library. A few months later, William found an old manuscript \u2014 not particularly valuable and unknown to the book world \u2014 that said a private letter written by Shakespeare contained a strange secret that existed until 1758, the year of the manuscript's discovery. The manuscript claimed that the Shakespeare letter was hidden inside the cover of a 1599 Jaggard edition of \"The Passionate Pilgrim.\" William was thrilled with this discovery, convinced that Sir Humphrey had never seen the manuscript. Out of a collector's greed, he encouraged Sir Humphrey to buy the manuscript without revealing its contents. He showed it to the then director of the Kingston Museum, Mr. Hamnett, believing he would not tell anyone. Mr. Hamnett dismissed his words as nonsense. But William was fascinated by the lost document mentioned in the manuscript, believing it had high historical, literary, and monetary value, and began searching for it \u2014 despite knowing that most of the first edition Jaggard's \"The Passionate Pilgrim\" had disappeared over three centuries, with only three surviving copies. [2324] \"He wrote to me about beating up Grivel,\" Dr. Siddra said weakly. \"I didn't know he had gone mad in pursuit of the document. Thankfully, I agreed to Mr. Weiss's proposal and recently came to America. I wanted to find William if I could, to stop another crime. So I took the early ship to New York and advertised in the personal column of the newspaper. William contacted me soon after, and he came to see me at the cheap hotel where I was staying under a false name. He told me he had rented a house in Westchester under the old pseudonym Dr. Alessis. He had found the book of Saxon, but unfortunately, he couldn't get it because Saxon's will stipulated that the book be donated to the British Museum, and he couldn't get it. He also told me he had hired a thief named Vera to steal the book from Saxon's house. But Vera got confused and stole a worthless fake book, which William later anonymously returned. He was so frustrated that he told me the museum was closed for renovation, and the Jaggard edition and other books were donated to the museum, and he had to break in. I thought he was getting carried away by greed, trying to persuade him to give up. But the situation was getting out of hand. I was almost becoming the museum's director. But William remained stubborn, and our conversation yielded no results, and he left.\" [2325] \"I guess one night you secretly visited your brother's house \u2014 you were the person the butler called 'the\n[2326] \"Yes, but it was no use. I was very worried and terrified. My situation was quite precarious, as you know.\" [2327] \"What about your imprisonment? How did that happen?\" grumbled Sam. [2328] Dr. Sedgwick bit his lip. \"I never imagined he would go so far. He caught me off guard and took me. It was my own brother! Last Friday, I received a letter at the Seneca Hotel inviting me to a secret meeting near Terreton, not at his own house. He was mysterious, and I didn't suspect him, because...\"\n[2329] \"He attacked you?\" asked Paulin sharply. [2330] \"Yes,\" said the man, his lips trembling. \"It was my own brother who kidnapped me! He gagged me, tied me up, and threw me into a dirty, stinking hole... The rest, you know\n[2331] \"But why?\" Sam asked, \"I don't understand.\" [2332] Dr. Sedlar shrugged his thin shoulders. \"I think he was afraid I would expose him. I threatened to call the police and catch him when I was angry. I think he didn't want me to get in his way before he left the country with the document.\" [2333] \"Your monocular was found in the Alais house. I now know he was murdered. Explain that.\" Sam said coldly. [2334] \"My monocular? Oh, right.\" He waved wearily, \"The newspaper made a big deal out of it. I can explain. William took it from me, and at the time... he said he was going back to the house to get the document - he hid it there - and then wanted to escape. But I guess he met the killer, and the monocular probably slipped out of his pocket, and he was beaten to death. There's no doubt he was killed because he had the document.\" [2335] \"So the document is in the hands of the killer who killed your brother?\" [2336] \"Anyone else?\" [2337] There was a brief silence. Donohue really did fall asleep, his snoring sounding like gunfire and adding to the feeling of stillness. Peckins and Rowe exchanged glances, both standing up and leaning against the sides of the bed. [2338] \"But what about the secret, Dr. Sedra?\" Rowe said, his eyes burning. [2339] \"You can't just leave it at that!\" Peckins exclaimed. [2340] The man on the bed smiled at the two of them and then whispered, \"So you want to know too? What if I told you that this secret was connected to... Shakespeare's death?\" [2341] \"Shakespeare's death!\" [2342] \"What, what?\" Rowe gruffly replied. [2343] \"But how can one write their own death?\" Perkins asked. [2344] \"A very important question,\" the British man chuckled, suddenly changing positions on the bed, his eyes ablaze with fire. \"How did Shakespeare die?\" [2345] \"No one knows,\" Rowe muttered. \"But there are some scientific speculations. I remember reading an article in an old issue of The Lancet that talked about Shakespeare's cause of death being a combination of several diseases\u2014typhoid fever, epilepsy, arteriosclerosis, chronic alcoholism, nephritis, spinal tuberculosis, and who knows what else. I believe there were thirteen in total.\" [2346] \"Really?\" Dr. Sedra whispered, \"Interesting. According to this old manuscript...,\" he paused, \"Shakespeare was murdered.\" [2347] A silence pressed down on them, making it hard to breathe. The Englishman wore a faint, odd smile on his face: \"This letter seems to be written by Shakespeare to a man named William Humphrey...\"\n[2348] \"Humphrey?\" Rowe whispered, \"William Humphrey? The only Humphrey I've heard of in relation to Shakespeare is Orazio Humphrey, who was hired in 1783 to make a charcoal portrait of Shakespeare. Mr. Lane, have you heard of this Humphrey?\" [2349] \"No.\" [2350] \"That's a new name for Shakespeare scholars,\" Sedra said, \"This\u2014\"\n[2351] \"Well, well,\" Rowe exclaimed excitedly, his eyes wide. \"W.H!\" [2352] \"What?\" [2353] \"W.H. From Shakespeare's sonnets!\" (1)\n[2354] \"That is an interesting idea. It could be. There's never been any certainty about it. But we do know: William Humphrey is a direct ancestor of Sir Humphrey!\" [2355] Pechinis was awestruck. \"That explains how this letter ended up in the Humphrey family.\" [2356] \"The right answer. Obviously, Humphrey was a close friend of the poet.\" [2357] Young Rowley jumped to the foot of the bed. \"You must explain this to me,\" he panted, \"What date was the letter written? When was it sent?\" [2358] \"April 22, 1616!\" [2359] \"Oh my! The day before Shakespeare died! You \u2013 you read the letter?\" [2360] \"Unfortunately, I didn't see it. But my brother told me, he couldn't resist\u2026\" Sedley sighed, \"Strange, isn't it? In the letter, he tells his friend William Humphrey that he 'is almost drowned,' that he is 'very weak,' and that he believes someone is slowly poisoning him. The next day \u2013 he died.\" [2361] \"Oh, my God!\" Rowe repeated, tugging at his collar as if he was being strangled. [2362] \"Poisoned, huh?\" The Inspector shook his head. \"Who would want to poison this old man?\" [2363] Percy stiffened. \"It seems we have to solve a three-hundred-year-old murder case, and then\u2026\"\n[2364] \"And then what, Percy?\" Rayne's voice was odd. [2365] She shuddered, avoiding his gaze, and turned away. [2366] \u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\n[2367] (1) When The Sonnets by Shakespeare was first published, it mentioned a person named W.H., but scholars were unable to determine who this person was. [2368] Chapter 28\n[2369] The Clue of the Bell\n[2370] Miss Percy Sam's sudden change in personality had the inspector worried. She ate like a bird, slept little, and spent her days shuttling between her apartment and the office at Sam's, looking like a thin, pale ghost with her mind on other things. She would occasionally complain of headaches and spend hours in her room. When she emerged, she still looked tired and despondent. [2371] \"What's the matter?\" the inspector asked one day slyly. \"Had a row with your boyfriend?\" [2372] \"With Gordon? No, Dad. We're just very good friends. And he's been so busy in Britain lately that I hardly see him.\" [2373] The inspector grumbled, watching her anxiously. Later that afternoon he called the museum and spoke to Gordon Rowe, but the young man sounded distracted and uninterested. The inspector hung up, feeling like a helpless father, but the next day he made Miss Braid's life very difficult. [2374] About a week after the incident at Tarrytown Hospital, Miss Percy appeared in her father's office in a new linen suit, looking more like her old self. [2375] \"I'm going for a drive,\" she said, pulling on her white gloves. \"Out in the country. Mind if I do, Dad?\" [2376] \"Oh, not at all. Have fun. Going alone?\" [2377] Percy looked at her reflection in the mirror. \"Of course. Why not?\" [2378] \"I thought \u2013 Rowe boy \u2013 Percy, he's been neglecting you, hasn't he?\" [2379] \"Dad! Absolutely not. He's \u2013 he's been very busy. And why should I care?\" [2380] As Percy climbed into the car parked below and started the engine, the indifferent expression vanished, and the wrinkles on her forehead deepened. She didn't glance at the British Museum on Fifth Avenue as she passed by, but when she stopped at the corner of Sixty-sixth Street to wait for the green light, she couldn't help but glance at the rearview mirror. Of course, there was nothing to see, and she sighed, continuing to drive forward. [2381] The journey to Thayerville was long and lonely. She held the steering wheel with her gloved hands, driving carelessly, her eyes on the road but her mind elsewhere. [2382] She stopped in front of a general store in the town center, went inside, and flipped through a phone book, asking a shopkeeper a question before leaving. She continued driving, turning into a narrow street and slowly sliding along, checking door numbers. Five minutes later, she found her target \u2013 a dilapidated bungalow with an untidy garden and a wobbly fence covered in vines. [2383] She walked up the porch, rang the doorbell, and the coarse ring echoed inside. A tired-looking middle-aged woman opened the screen door, wearing rumpled clothes and with red, soapy hands. [2384] \"What is it?\" she snapped, eyeing Percy with a guarded hostility. [2385] \"Is Mr. Maxwell at home?\" [2386] \"Which one?\" she asks. [2387] \"There's more than one? I mean the one who was the butler for Dr. Ales recently.\" [2388] \"Oh, my brother,\" the woman huffs. \"Wait here. I'll see if he's around.\" [2389] The woman disappears, and Percy sighs, settling into a dusty rocking chair. [2390] A few moments later, a tall, elderly Maxwell emerges\u2014he's pulling a sweat-soaked shirt over his thin, bare neck. [2391] \"Miss Sam!\" he croaked, glancing around the street to see if anyone else was there, \"Are you here to see me?\" [2392] \"Hello, Mr. Maxwell,\" Percy said cheerfully, \"No, I'm alone. Can I sit down?\" He sat in an old, wobbly chair with peeling paint like burnt skin. He looked at her anxiously. [2393] \"I guess you heard about the explosion?\" [2394] \"Yes, Miss! Terrible. I told my brother and sister-in-law how lucky I was. If you two hadn't come to get me out of there, I'd have been turned into hamburger.\" [2395] \"I don't think they've found out who did it yet.\" Percy looked at him seriously, \"Mr. Maxwell, I've been thinking about this case a lot, especially what you said. I can't help but think you must have left something out!\" [2396] He was surprised. \"Oh, no! I swear I'm telling the truth!\" [2397] \"I'm not saying you're lying on purpose. Be careful of that bee... I mean, you might have missed something very important.\" [2398] He ran his fingers through his hair nervously. \"Oh, I don't know.\" [2399] \"Listen,\" Perkins said, brightening up, \"everyone \u2013 except me \u2013 obviously overlooked one thing. The masked man locked you in the garage, the walls were thin, only a few feet from the front door of the house. It was night, in the countryside, and any sound could be heard clearly.\" [2400] \"Damn!\" he breathed, widening his eyes, \"I did hear it!\" [2401] Perkins rushed into her father's office, where Jules Renard sat glumly staring out the window at Times Square. [2402] \"Is this - a meeting?\" Perkins took off her gloves, her eyes conveying that she had news. [2403] The young Mr. Rowe turned around and strode forward. \"Petty! I've been worried sick. Are you okay?\" [2404] \"Fine, thank you.\" Perkins replied coolly, \"I - \"\n[2405] \"My luck is terrible.\" The young man said despondently, \"I've hit a dead end. No progress on the case, Petty.\" [2406] \"Interesting.\" [2407] \"Yes!\" he sat down in front of her, adopting a thoughtful pose, \"I was all wrong, off on the wrong track. My great Shakespearean study plan is now a bust. Oh, so many months, so many years\u2026\"\n[2408] Paisley sighed, her expression softening. \"I'm sorry, Gordon. I don't know \u2013 poor man.\" [2409] The chief inspector growled, \"Don't beat around the bush! Where have you been? We didn't wait for you.\" [2410] \"Where?\" [2411] \"To see Sedra. Mr. Reane thought of something. Reane, you'd better hurry up.\" [2412] The old gentleman looked sharply at Percy. \"We can wait. Percy, what is it? You seem to be trying to suppress your excitement.\" [2413] \"Is that so?\" Percy smiled nervously, \"I'm not good at acting. I just discovered something most incredible.\" [2414] \"Maxwell? Why?\" The sergeant was skeptical. [2415] \"I didn't ask him thoroughly last time. I remembered something, no one asked him\u2026 He knows how many people entered the Alessi house on the night of the murder.\" [2416] \"So what?\" Reine paused. \"If true, that's interesting. How so?\" [2417] \"The house was ransacked by the masked man, and Maxwell was in the garage the whole time, fully conscious. I remember an old-fashioned bell above the front door that would ring every time it opened.\" [2418] \"Ah!\" [2419] \"I'm sure Maxwell heard the bell \u2013 every time! I asked him, and he remembered hearing it. It doesn't seem important\u2026\"\n[2420] \"Child, you are incredibly clever.\" Reine whispered. [2421] \"I was stupid earlier not to think of it. Anyway, Maxwell went over the events in his mind. The masked man locked him in the garage, then took Maxwell's key and went back into the house. Maxwell clearly heard the bell ring twice, with a very short interval, only a few seconds.\" [2422] \"Twice?\" Sam asked, \"The opening and closing of the door.\" [2423] \"That's right. This means the masked man was alone in the house. It was quiet after that \u2013 Maxwell guessed at least half an hour passed before there were two more quick bell sounds. Then two more, which were the last sounds Maxwell heard that night.\" [2424] \"I think that's enough.\" Rayn said with an odd tone. [2425] \"Good work, Darling!\" Rowe called, \"You're so smart. This is progress! As you said, the first two bell sounds indicate the masked man entered the house. The second time indicates the second person entered. The third time indicates that one of them left. There were no more bells, so there were only two people in the house during the murder \u2013 the masked man and the mystery guest!\" [2426] \"I guess that's it, Gordon,\" Perkins called. \"Just like I thought. From the clock evidence, we know the masked man was the axe-wielder; and according to the watch on the corpse and the cuts on the wrist, obviously the axe-wielder was the murderer. So the mystery man was the victim, killed and dumped in the cellar.\" [2427] \"Reduced to two,\" Lane said coldly. \"Inspector, the mystery is cleared up, isn't it?\" [2428] \"Wait a minute,\" Sam said. \"Miss, don't rush. How do you know the second bell sound was made by the second person? How do you know it wasn't made by the masked man when he left the house? And the third bell sound caused by the second person \u2013\"\n[2429] Perkins didn't let him finish. \"No. Can't you see it's impossible? We know someone was killed in the house during that time. Who was it? If the second person went in after the masked man left, what would have happened? A murder without a murderer.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[1715] The inspector strode back, barking, \"Vera!\" \u2013 the thief immediately tensed \u2013 \"My good child, come with me!\" [1716] \"You want - you want to take me where?\" [1717] \"You'll find out soon enough.\" - the scholars had stopped talking and were looking anxiously and seriously at Inspector Sam - \"Dr. Sedra, are you staying here?\" [1718] \"What did you say?\" the Briton whispered, surprised. [1719] \"We're going to check out Dr. Aless's den.\" The inspector grinned slyly. \"Maybe you'd like to come along too.\" [1720] \"Hey -\" Vera swallowed. [1721] Dr. Sedra frowned. \"I'm afraid I don't understand your meaning.\" [1722] \"I have many things to discuss with Dr. Sedra today,\" Dr. Jott impatiently said. [1723] \"Ah, so,\" Reine suddenly stood up, \"Officer, forget it. Dr. Sedra must not be fond of American enthusiasm after this terrible incident. And, Dr., where do you live? In case we need to find you urgently \u2014\"\n[1724] \"The Seneca Hotel, Mr. Reine.\" [1725] \"Thank you. Let's go, Officer. Percy, Gordon, it seems we can't get rid of you.\" The old gentleman chuckled, \"Ah, curious young people.\" He shook his head thoughtfully and headed towards the door. [1726] Chapter 19\n[1727] The Mysterious House\n[1728] With the reluctant Italian leading the way, Dromio drives the Lincoln away from the main road and into a narrow path between the towns of Ephesus and Tirrano. This little road is made of gravel, with low-hanging branches on both sides. They suddenly emerge from the dull world of concrete into the cool countryside. Birds and insects flutter above their heads, and there is no one around. The path winds and turns, passing through the greenery. [1729] \"Are you sure this is the place?\" Sam asks impatiently. [1730] Vera nods cautiously. \"I believe so.\" [1731] They seemed to be going through endless forests, and everyone was silent and pale. Finally, they were going to see Dr. Alessandri! The past few weeks of confusion were about to be cleared up. They watched the trees rush by anxiously. [1732] The leaves disappeared, and they came to another path - the first exit they had met after leaving the main road a mile away. This path was a driveway, snaking off to the left, through the dull, scrubby bushes, to a place about 150 feet away where there seemed to be a house. From the gaps in the trees, you could see the sloping roofs, broken and ragged. [1733] \"Stop here,\" Vera said. \"This is it. Now I can -\"\n[1734] \"Sit down,\" the sergeant glared at him, then turned to Dromio. \"Don't get nervous, buddy. Be careful now, don't scare anyone. Keep quiet.\" [1735] Dromio handled the steering wheel as if he were holding feathers, slipping the car into a narrow alley next to it. The car crept gently forward, and the alley widened a bit, then a small clearing appeared, and before them stood a dilapidated house, weather-beaten and now a dirty grayish-yellow. The paint was peeling, making the whole building look like a peeled potato. There was a small porch in front of the house, with rotten wooden steps that had sunk in. The door was sturdy, and all the windows had shutters tightly closed. The branches of the trees brushed against the walls. To the left of the house stood an old wooden shed, and about ten feet away from it was a shaky house, apparently a garage, with both doors closed. Phone lines and cables stretched mysteriously from the house and the garage into the wild. [1736] \"What a lovely ruin!\" Percy exclaimed. [1737] \"Shh!\" The sergeant said sternly, \"Alright, Dromio. The rest of you stay here. Joe, when I go to scout ahead, don't get any bright ideas. If you cooperate, I promise you won't suffer.\" [1738] He quickly climbed out of the car, crossed the open ground, stepped onto the porch, and despite his bulky frame, his movements were surprisingly light. The paint on the door was just as weathered as the wall paint, but it was still a sturdy piece of wood. There was a small bell next to the door. He didn't ring the bell, but tried to look through the windows, only to find that the tightly closed shutters didn't allow him to peek inside. He lightly descended the steps and disappeared to the left of the house. Three minutes later, he reappeared from the right, shaking his head. [1739] \"Damn it, it seems like an abandoned house. Alright, let's take a look!\" He strode confidently onto the porch and pressed the doorbell with force. [1740] For a moment - so quickly that it seemed he must have been peering through a crack - a man opened the door and stepped out. As soon as the door opened, a bell rang above it. The old-fashioned device hung above the door, and as soon as the door moved slightly, the spring attached to the bell would vibrate and jump. The man was tall and old, with a pale face covered in wrinkles. His cloudy gray eyes briefly rested on the sergeant, then looked at the sunlit carriage, and then back at him. [1741] \"Sir, is there something you need?\" His voice was shrill, \"Can I be of service?\" [1742] \"Is this the house of Dr. Aleis?\" [1743] The old man eagerly nodded, looking somewhat pleased. He smiled and scratched his head. \"Oh, yes, sir. Do you have news of him? I've been worried \u2013\"\n[1744] \"Ah, I see. Wait here,\" the sergeant walked to the edge of the porch, \"you all better come up. It seems we'll be waiting for a while.\" [1745] The emaciated old man led them through the narrow corridor to a small living room. The interior was cool. The furniture in the room was old and shiny, with an old carpet and old paintings. A pungent odor reminiscent of a funeral home filled the air. The old man busily opened the shutters and curtains, and the room seemed even more shabby and repulsive under the sunlight. [1746] The sergeant coldly said, \"First, we want to know who you are?\" [1747] The old man cheerfully smiled, \"Sir, my name is Maxwell. I take care of the house for Dr. Alley, doing all sorts of chores \u2014 cooking, cleaning, chopping wood, buying 