gh they might not fill their spirits, they could definitely fill their stomachs. During the meal, no one spoke much, Percy was unusually silent, Rowley was full of thoughts, and Rene said some trivial things without mentioning the morning's events. After lunch, he handed the guests over to Quincy and retired to the study. [2031] After lunch, he handed the guests over to Quincey and retired to the study himself. Percy and Rowe wandered around the spacious Hamlet Manor. They strolled through the charming garden and lay comfortably on the quiet grass. Quincey saw them, chuckled, and disappeared. [2032] Birds chirped, and the grass smelled sweet and fragrant. Both were silent. Rowe turned to look at his companion's face. In the warm sunlight, her cheeks were rosy, and her slender figure was relaxed and graceful. He looked at her warmly, feeling strange; she was both captivating and unattainable. Her eyes were closed, and a fine line appeared between her eyebrows, refusing to joke or flirt. [2033] Rowe sighed. \"Patty, what are you thinking? Please don't frown like that! I like my woman to be ordinary.\" [2034] She murmured, \"Am I frowning?\" Then she opened her eyes and smiled at him. \"Gordon, you're a child. I've been thinking \u2013\"\n[2035] \"I think I have to marry a wife with a sharp mind,\" the young man said lightly. \"The point is, I'm sharp-minded too \u2013 so there would be two of us\u2026\"\n[2036] \"Oh\u2014\" Rowe suddenly lay back on the grass, plucking a blade of grass. [2037] She sat up, her eyes gentle. \"So you figured it out too, Gordon? One of them was the axe-wielder. The state of the house showed that he was looking for something; he didn't know where it was, and he was determined to find it. He was systematically destroying furniture and other things with an axe. Most importantly, this person was not Dr. Ales.\" [2038] Rowe yawned. \"Of course not. If he was Ales, he would have known where to find his own things. The secret cabinet on the wall