or of the British Museum. But is the person who appeared at the British Museum and claimed to be Sedley really Hamnet Sedley? Or is it, as Dad thought, that someone was pretending to be Hamnet Sedley? He must have something he doesn't want to be known, and he lied about the date he arrived. Did Hamnet Sedley die? Did this person steal his name and take his place? Why did he hide the date of his arrival? What did he do between the actual arrival time and the fabricated arrival time?\" [2051] \"Ah!\" Young Rowe said, \"What a profound thought!\" [2052] Perkins glared at him and continued to read aloud: \"\uff083\uff09If Hamnet Sedley is not Dr. Alessi, what happened to Dr. Alessi? Why did he disappear?\" [2053] \"\uff084\uff09What happened to Donohue?\" [2054] \"\uff085\uff09Who chased me and Gordon and took the envelope?\" [2055] \"\uff086\uff09The axe-wielder is who? He is not Dr. Alessi, but someone else.\" [2056] \"\uff08Seven\uff09 Who followed the axeman and opened the secret cabinet? It could be Dr. Aless himself \u2013 he would know where he hid things.\" [2057] \"Wait a minute, Pechins,\" said Lane. \"How do you know the axeman isn't Dr. Aless? And there were two people in the Aless house last night?\" [2058] \"No, there's one more,\" Pechins frowned. \"The most important and puzzling question.\" She continued, \"\uff08Eight\uff09 What do these points point to? There's no doubt that Dr. Aless's 'million-dollar secret' is involved. But the million-dollar secret is related to the symbol Dr. Aless left for my father, so the solution to everything depends on the last question: what does the symbol mean?\" [2059] After she finished speaking, she put down the paper and began doodling on the table. The two men were silent for a while.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:46:09 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"Because there's a lunatic who insisted you be present when we opened the letter. Dad, open it! If you don't, I'll go mad.\" [1069] This was the long brown paper envelope that the eccentric gentleman with the rainbow moustache had deposited in the inspector's safe about seven weeks ago. [1070] Rayne took the envelope from Sam, feeling its weight. As he felt the rectangular envelope inside, his eyes narrowed. \"This mysterious action needs an explanation. I'd like to know more... no, no, dear, I've told you before to cultivate \u2014 ha, ha \u2014 the virtue of patience. Inspector, please proceed!\" [1071] Sam briefly recounted the story of the disguised British visitor on May 6th. Pencin added details as he went along, making the story quite complete and particularly detailed in describing the visitor. After the sergeant finished, Rine looked thoughtfully at the envelope. \"But why didn't you tell me about this beforehand? Sergeant, that's not like you.\" [1072] \"I didn't think it was necessary. Well, let's get on with it!\" [1073] \"Wait a minute, your mysterious customer didn't call you yesterday as he was supposed to?\" [1074] \"But he called on May 20th,\" the sergeant said, exasperated. [1075] \"We've been sitting here all day,\" Pencin grumbled. \"Until midnight yesterday. No word from him. Now\u2014\"\n[1076] \"Do you have a record of your conversation with this person?\" Lanyon said mildly. \"I know you have a recorder here.\" [1077] Sam pressed a button. \"Miss Broadly, bring in the conversation record for the envelope case.\" [1078] They sat there anxiously, waiting for the old man to meticulously read their conversation record with the visitor. [1079] \"Hmm.\" He put down the report. \"Very strange. Yes, the guy was deliberately disguised. Clumsy, very clumsy! Clearly unrealistic. That beard\u2026\" He shook his head. \"Alright, Inspector, I think we can start now. This is your honor.\" [1080] He stood up, threw the envelope on Sam's desk, sat down in the chair next to the desk, leaned forward, and looked focused. Percy quickly circled the desk and stood behind her father's chair. Her breathing was rapid, and her usually calm face was pale and agitated. Sam used trembling fingers to pull out the pad near Lanyon and put the envelope on it. He was sweating profusely, then looked up at Lanyon\u2014they were sitting across the table from each other\u2014and smiled helplessly. [1081] \"Okay, let's start,\" he said jokingly. \"I hope nothing jumps out and says 'April Fool's Day' or something like that.\" [1082] Percy, standing behind him, sighed in frustration because she couldn't breathe due to her nervousness. [1083] The sergeant held the letter opener, hesitated for a moment, then inserted the knife into the seal of the brown paper envelope. He deftly cut the seal, put down the knife, and peered inside the envelope. [1084] \"What is it?\" Percy exclaimed. [1085] \"Percy, you're right,\" he muttered. \"It's another envelope. \" He took out a small square envelope - light gray, also sealed, with a blank front. [1086] \"What's on the envelope?\" The old gentleman asked hastily. [1087] The inspector flipped the envelope over, his face turning ashen. [1088] Percy, standing behind him, was gasping for breath as he observed the label on the envelope. [1089] Sam licked his lips. \"It says... His voice was hoarse, \"It says... Oh my... It says: Saxon Library!\" [1090] This was the first sign - indicating that the mysterious guest with the rainbow beard might be connected to the bizarre events at the British Museum. [1091] \"The Saxon Library,\" said Reine, \"How odd.\" [1092] \"Well, I'll be damned,\" Sam exclaimed, \"What have we stumbled upon?\" [1093] The old man spoke laboriously, \"Obviously a coincidence, Inspector. Such things happen, but this coincidence is enough to make one believe...,\" his voice trailed off. He did not move his gaze from the inspector's lips, but it seemed his eyes did not see anything, for there was a glow over them, like a veil had fallen\u2014a veil that hid the sudden insight that had entered his eyes. [1094] \"But I don't understand...,\" Percy said, bewildered. [1095] Reine shuddered, the veil disappeared. \"Inspector, open it!\" he said, leaning forward, his hands resting on his chin. [1096] Sam picked up the letter opener again and inserted the blade into the seal, slowly applying pressure. The paper was stiff and reluctant to yield. [1097] Raine and Percy did not blink an eye. [1098] Sam's thick fingers reached into the envelope and pulled out a sheet of pale gray paper, the same tone as the envelope, neatly folded. He unfolded the paper. There was some text printed at one end. The inspector turned the paper over, and the text on the other side was simple: Saxon Library. The text was gray, a darker shade than the paper. He laid the paper flat on the pad, placing it between himself and Raine. They both stared at the paper, and the office fell into silence. [1099] The reason was: if the imposter was a mysterious figure, the secret he held in the inspector's hand was even more mysterious - not only mysterious, but also eerie. There was no logic in this at all. [1100] The letter had \"Saxon Library\" printed at the top, and the rest of the page was as smooth as freshly printed paper, with only a few words, or symbols, written near the center, just below the printed heading. [1101] Nothing more. No decipherable information, no signature, no mark made by pen or pencil. [1102] The inspector's old body suddenly trembled with excitement, hunched over in his chair, eyes fixed on the characters on the paper. The paper slipped from his fingers. Percy remained motionless. For a long time, no one moved. Eventually, the old man slowly shifted his gaze from the paper to Sam. In the depths of his clear eyes, there was a hint of triumph, almost a gleam of euphoria. [1103] However, the inspector muttered, \"3HS wM.\" His tone was bewildered, the syllables rolling off his tongue, as if he were trying to unravel the meaning hidden within the sounds. [1104] Ren's face showed a touch of confusion, glancing quickly at Percy. [1105] She murmured, \"3HS wM,\" as if a child repeating foreign language. [1106] The old man buried his face in his hands and sat there motionless. [1107] * * *\n[1108] \"Well!\" The inspector finally sighed deeply, \"I give up, damn it, I give up. A guy walks in dressed like a clown in a parade and leaves a bunch of crazy talk about 'a million-dollar secret' - tell you, I give up. It's a joke, someone's joke.\" [1109] Pechin swiftly circled around her father's chair and picked up the paper. She furrowed her brow and concentrated on deciphering the meaning of the symbols. The inspector pushed his chair back to the window and watched the bustling Times Square outside. [1110] Philo Rine suddenly looked up and quietly asked, \"Pechin, let me take a look, please?\" [1111] Pechin sat down, bewildered, as the old man took the note from her hand and examined the cryptic text. [1112] The symbol was hastily written, resembling calligraphy, and ink that was likely the darkest. The fluidity and confidence of the strokes indicated no hesitation, as the writer clearly knew what he was writing and executed it with one stroke. [1113] Ren put down the note, picked up the gray envelope and examined it, flipping it over and over again. The words \"Saxon Library\" printed on the seal seemed to fascinate him. He felt the seal with his fingertips, the black letters printed on it shining brightly, touching his fingertips' sensory nerves. [1114] He put down the envelope, closed his eyes, and leaned back, murmuring, \"Sergeant, this is not a joke.\" [1115] Sam slid his chair. \"So what the hell does it mean? If it's not a joke, it must mean something... damn it, he said it was just a 'clue.' He wasn't wrong, it's the most confusing clue I've ever seen. Intentionally complicated, huh? Hmmph!\" He went back to the window. [1116]Percy furrowed his brow again. \"It can't be that hard to understand. He probably wanted to make it mysterious. He should have made it simpler so we could figure it out after careful study. Let me see... maybe it's some kind of abbreviation, right? Containing some information?\" [1117]The inspector sighed without turning around. [1118]\"Or,\" Percy continued to ponder, \"it could be chemical symbols. H for hydrogen, right? And S for sulfur. Hydrogen-sulfur. Right!\u201d\n[1119]\"No,\" Reynolds said in a low voice, \"I think the molecular formula for hydrogen sulfide is H2S. It seems that HS has nothing to do with chemistry, Percy.\" [1120]\"There's still...\" Percy said dejectedly, \"the small w and the capital M... Oh, God, it's confusing. I wish Gordon were here; he knows a lot of useless things.\" [1121] The sergeant slowly turned around. \"Of course, no headway.\u201d His tone was odd, \"For us, that is, Petty; for your lively Mr. Rowe, too. But don't forget, this mysterious person involves Reynolds, so he may think Reynolds would know what it means\u2026 Hey, Reynolds?\u201d\n[1122] Ren remained indifferent to this obvious challenge, and then wrinkles appeared at the corners of his eyes. \"Suspect me? Maybe it is as you think, old man.\" [1123] \"Oh, so what the hell does that mean?\" the officer asked bluntly, walking over. [1124] Ren waved his fair hand and continued to stare at the paper in front of him, muttering, \"It's strange, I believe he thought you would also know what this means.\" [1125] The sergeant flushed and stood up, walking towards the door. \"Miss Brodie! Bring in the ledger.\" [1126] Miss Broadie quickly entered, holding a pencil. [1127] \"Write a letter to Dr. Leo Shilling of the Laboratory. 'Dear Doctor: Please attend to this matter immediately. Do not disclose. Does the following series of symbols have any special meaning to you?' Then write this: '3, capital H, capital S, small space, lowercase w, capital M'. Understood?\" [1128] Miss Broadie looked up, dumbfounded. \"Understood, sir.\" [1129] \"Send a copy of the same letter to Rupert Sheff, head of the decoding group at the Washington D.C. Intelligence Bureau. Hurry up.\" [1130] Miss Broadie quickly left. [1131] The inspector said confidently, \"We should get some results.\" [1132] He sat back in his chair, lit a cigar, stretched his thick legs, and puffed a cloud of smoke toward the ceiling thoughtfully. \"The first thing to investigate, in my opinion, is the text printed at the top of the note. This fellow walks in and tells us a story that no one believes, leaves a note full of nonsense. He doesn't want us to know he's connected to Saxen, so he slipped the envelope into an unmarked brown paper bag. If anything happens to him, he wants us to open the envelope. He wants us to see the words 'Saxen Library' and investigate in that direction. So far, it seems clear.\" [1133] Rine nodded. \"I agree with your assessment.\" [1134] \"What he didn't count on was George Fisher coming here to tell me about Donohue, which led us to the British Museum and the ridiculous story of the stolen book. What's the connection between him and Saxen? I have no idea. Maybe the appearance of the Saxen note was just a coincidence.\" [1135] \"It's not a coincidence, Dad,\" Perkins said wearily. \"I don't believe it. I believe the man with the fake mustache and the British oddities are connected. The symbols on the Saxen library note are the point of connection. I think\u2014\"\n[1136] \"What?\" Sam looked shrewdly at his daughter. [1137] Perkins smiled. \"It's a silly idea, but the whole thing is crazy... I was thinking, what if the guy with the fake mustache... what if he was someone pretending to be from the Saxton family!\" [1138] \"They wouldn't be that stupid,\" the sergeant said nonchalantly. \"But Perkins, I have similar thoughts. You see, Rowley... \"\n[1139] \"Nonsense!\" Perkins exclaimed sharply, and both men looked at her. \"No! It couldn't be Gordon.\" [1140] \"Why not?\" Sam asked. \"I thought he