e ribbon, the Chief broke the silence: \"Peachins, tell us what's going on. Obviously, Rene is in trouble. I don't know you, so you should have told me earlier\u2014\"\n[2601] \"Yeah,\" her voice was hoarse, \"it's my fault... Dad, it's unfair not to let you know. And you, Gordon. It's important for both of you to know. Gordon, drive faster! There's... there's a disaster ahead!\" [2602] Roarke's lips tightened. The sports car sped forward like a frightened hare. [2603] \"In the end...,\" Perkins said, her nose twitching, \"you saw it too. We came to the conclusion that the victim and the killer were the Sedra brothers. We thought one of them killed the other in the house. But then things changed. Last week - at the museum - things changed. We found out that the dead in the rubble was Hamnet, and the survivor was his brother William, and William couldn't have been one of the two people who entered the house that night. You remember how I proved that - with the key. That means our reasoning doesn't hold up; we know the victim was Hamnet Sedra, but we don't know who was the first person to enter the house that night, the kidnapper of Maxwell, the axe-wielder... The thought of it scares me. I remember some forgotten things that happened or I saw, that didn't make sense at the time, but later became - like a lightning bolt - terrifyingly clear.\" [2604] She looked ahead at the road. \"The last key to the whole problem is to find out who was the first person to enter the house. What happened then? This person tied Maxwell up, stuffed his mouth, took Maxwell's duplicate key, and went back into the house. The door closed automatically behind him because it had a spring lock. He took a small axe from the wooden box in the kitchen and attacked the study. Obviously, in theory, the study was the most likely place to hide the files he was looking for. He didn't know where the files might be hidden in the study; he hacked everything ruthlessly, that's the evidence. First, he might have gone through all the books, guessing that the folder was in one of them. Not finding it, he hacked the furnishings of the house - the paneling, the floor, etc. At midnight - we knew from the clock's hands - he destroyed the clock, I guess he thought it might be hiding the files. He was clueless, he couldn't find the files in the study, nor anywhere else on the first floor. So he went upstairs to William Sedra's bedroom, because that was the second most likely place to hide something.\" [2605] \"We know all that, Petty.\" Sam looked at her strangely. [2606] \"Dad, don't worry... From the broken bedroom clock, we know he was in the bedroom at 12:24. Alright, according to Hamnet's watch, he was killed in the house at 12:26 - just two minutes after the axeman broke the clock in the upstairs bedroom. The question is: When did Hamnet enter the house? He had to open the door, walk into the study, see the mess, climb up to the hollow wall panel above the bookshelf, take out the document, climb down the ladder, possibly check the document, and then encounter the murderer, struggle, and be killed. Obviously, this whole process took more than two minutes! So Hamnet must have entered the house while the axeman was still inside.\" [2607] \"So what?\" The officer's voice grew stern. [2608] \"I'm about to get there,\" said Percy calmly. \"William Sedgwick said that Hamnet wanted to destroy the documents. Once Hamnet got the documents in the study, what would he do? He would immediately destroy them. How would he do it? Fire would be the quickest and most convenient method. He would have struck a match, holding the document in his hand, and started putting the paper on the flame.\u201d She sighed. \"Of course, this is only theoretical and not very meaningful, but it clears up one point. This explains the axe marks on Hamnet's wrist and watch! Because if just as Hamnet was about to bring the matchstick close to the document, he was caught by the axe-wielder - who wanted to save the document and not destroy it - in a state of panic, he would naturally attack Hamnet to prevent the document from being destroyed by fire. So he swung his axe at Hamnet's hand like lightning, cutting his wrist and watch, forcing the destroyer to let go of the document and the matchstick. Undoubtedly, Hamnet also resisted. In the struggle, the axe-wielder shot him. The whole struggle may have started in the study, where the axe-wielder put down the axe and slowly moved towards the corridor, where we found Hamnet's single-lens glasses, where he may have been shot... The axe-wielder dragged Hamnet's body down to the cellar, unaware of the bomb there, and then, if the document had not been destroyed before he swung his axe at Hamnet's wrist, he picked up the document and left the house. From the traces of hacking and struggling, the axe-wielder seemed to go to any lengths - physical combat, murder - to preserve the document.\" [2609] Hamlet Manor is situated on the cliff above, and Rowe is fully concentrated on driving along the steep road. As he skillfully wrestles with the winding road, Percy remains silent. Suddenly, the manor appears before them. The car passes through the odd little bridge, and the tires sing along the gravel path. [2610] Luo Wei furrowed his brows and asked, \"Even if all of these are true, Petty, I still don't understand what the outcome is. The traces of the killer are still as elusive as ever.\" [2611] Rowe frowns. \"Even if all this is true, Peri, I still don't understand what the result is. The killer's tracks are still as elusive as ever.\" [2612] \"You think so?\" Perkins exclaimed, closing her eyes and shivering slightly, like a child swallowing bitter medicine. \"This is all very clear, as clear as - original sin! This man's features - his features, Gordon - what happened in the house exposed his identity!\" [2613] The two men look at her in confusion. They are now passing through the gate and slowly descending the winding driveway. Quixi's small figure and leathery face emerge from the lilac bushes, squinting and then smiling, revealing countless wrinkles. He waves and jumps onto the road. [2614] Rowe stops the car. [2615] \"Quixi!\" Perkins' voice stiffens, standing slightly between the two men. \"How is Mr. Lane?\" [2616] \"Letter?\" Sam said, puzzled, \"Strange. Here you go.\" Quincey handed him an envelope, which he tore open. [2617] \"Letter?\" Peachins was equally bewildered, sitting back between the two men and staring at the blue sky, saying, \"Thank God, he's alright.\" [2618] The inspector had been quietly reading the letter when his eyebrows furrowed into a deep crease and he read aloud in a loud voice -\n[2619] Dear Inspector:\n[2620] I believe that Peachins has ended her terrifying experience and has returned home safely. I know that my \"special statement\" will safely bring her home. While you were waiting, you may have hoped to find some answers to the mysteries - that have troubled you in your investigation - to distract yourself. [2621] The main question, as Percy and Gordon pointed out, is why a sensible, intelligent, and learned man like Hamnet Sadler would want to destroy a manuscript written by the hand of Shakespeare himself, which is so rare and invaluable that it cannot be replaced? I have myself unraveled this mystery, and can now tell you the answer. [2622] This manuscript is a letter to the ancestor of Sir Humphrey, who was a close friend of the poet. The author - Shakespeare - not only tells him that he suspects himself of being slowly poisoned, but also mentions the name of the suspicious poisoner... This is a very strange world, Shakespeare accuses the person named Hamnet Sadler. Inspector, Hamnet Sadler is the direct ancestor of the Sadler brothers. [2623] Strange, isn't it? That's why a scholar, a learned man, a sincere and wise antiquarian, a proud American, would defy his instincts of education and science, and even destroy something that could become the most precious treasure in the world, to hide this from the world: Shakespeare - Carlyle (3) praised the poet as having \"the greatest wisdom\"; Ben Jonson said he was \"not only of the moment, but of the ages\" - a man who has been worshipped and revered by people for three hundred years, has been murdered by the ancestor of Hamnet Sadler; even more terrifying, the ancestor and the name are the same! Some people may find a touch of madness in his enthusiasm, while others refuse to believe it; but the pride of the ancestors and the aging are incurable diseases, burning in the cold flame... William was not infected by this disease, his scientific spirit overcame them. But he still couldn't avoid the secular entanglement, to seize the document for himself and not pass it on to posterity. The third person in this case, the main character who appeared only once in the murder of the night, was willing to sacrifice his life to preserve the document for the world. [2624] Tell Percy, Gordon, and anyone else interested - the truth will soon be revealed to the world, old friend - they don't have to worry about the safety of the document. I personally took care of it and sent it back to its rightful owner in Britain - it is legally British property and spiritually belongs to the whole world - because the legal owner, Sir Humphrey, is no longer alive, he has no heirs, and his property has been donated to the royal family. If I can protect this work, Inspector, I know my friend will always remember my kindness. Like the self-importance of the worldly, I will think that even in the last stage of my life, I can still do something for humanity. [2625] Percy and Gordon, forgive this old man for paying attention to your intimate relationship, I think you two will be very happy together. You have similar interests, equal talents, and are promising young people, I know you will respect each other. May God bless you. I haven't forgotten you. [2626] My dear Detective, I am old and tired, and it seems that there is nothing... I am about to leave - I think, to take a long rest. Because I leave without anyone by my side, and you don't know, so I say these beautiful words to say goodbye:\n[2627] They say he left peacefully, having done his duty, so may heaven be with him! [2628] Until we meet again...\n[2629] Philo Vance\n[2630] Detective, he wrinkled his nose. \"I don't understand...\"\n[2631] Rowe glanced around quickly, and the peaceful, gleaming towers of Hamlet Manor shone above the treetops. [2632] Percy's voice tightened: \"Quincannon, where is Mr. Rain?\" [2633] Quincannon's little frog eyes brightened. \"Sunbathing in the West Garden, Miss Sam. I'm sure he'll be surprised to see you, I know he wasn't expecting any guests today.\" [2634] The two men jumped out of the car, and Percy stiffly stepped onto the gravel path, walking between them. Quince quietly followed behind. Percy began to walk across the lush, green grass towards the West Garden. [2635] \"You know...\" her voice was barely audible, and they had to strain their ears, \"the axe man revealed himself. He didn't make a mistake, he knew he didn't make a mistake - fate made the mistake for him, fate disguised as a cheap alarm clock.\" [2636] \"Alarm clock?\" the policeman muttered. [2637] \"When we searched the study, we saw Maxwell's alarm clock on the mantelpiece, still set to the time. What does this mean? The alarm clock rang at the appointed time - midnight twelve - because we checked it the next morning after Maxwell set the time, that is, before noon twelve. Do you remember we searched it and the timer rod was pointing to 'open'. If we saw the timer rod set to 'open', then the alarm clock must have stopped ringing. What does it mean for the alarm clock to ring? If it stopped, we saw the timer rod still pointing to 'open', which means it must have rung until it stopped. If it was turned off when it rang, then the timer rod wouldn't be pointing to 'open', but to 'closed'. So, the alarm clock wasn't turned off. It rang and rang until the spring inside broke, and the timer rod still pointed to 'open...'\n[2638] \"But what does this mean, Petty?\" Luo Wei called out. [2639] \"This explains everything. We know the axeman was in the room at midnight, so when the alarm clock started ringing, he must have been there too. We know this from two things: Maxwell said he always kept the clocks in the house running on time, and the floor clock was broken at exactly twelve o'clock.\" [2640] Luo Wei took a step back, silent, pale-faced. [2641] \"Okay, I'm listening,\" the officer called nervously. \"But why didn't your axe murderer turn off the alarm when it rang? He must have been shocked. In any case, anyone sneaking around in someone else's house would jump up, turn it off, whether anyone heard it or not.\" [2642] They stopped under an old oak tree, and Percy absent-mindedly stroked the rough bark. \"That's right,\" she said softly. \"The fact is, even if he was in the same room, and every instinct would have urged him to turn off the alarm, he didn't do it.\" [2643] Sam grumbled, \"I don't get it. Let's go, Gordon.\" He walked past the tree, and the others followed slowly. Not far away, they saw Raine sitting quietly on a wooden bench, facing away from them. [2644] Percy made a sad choking sound, and the officer quickly turned around. Roche's eyes were blank, and he rushed forward, putting his hand on her waist. [2645] \"What's going on?\" the officer asked slowly. [2646] \"Dad, wait,\" Percy cried, tears streaming down her face. \"Wait. You don't understand. You still don't understand. The axe-wielder dragged Hamnet Sedley's body into the cellar without hearing the ticking of the bomb. Why did he have to chop open the wall panel? He was obviously looking for a hollow spot. What is the normal procedure when looking for a hollow spot? Tap lightly! Tap lightly! Dad! Why didn't he tap those wall panels?\" [2647] Sam looked at Percy, then at Gordon, then with 